## 3. The Three Graph Divisors

Let `J=K3 x E` and let `Gamma_i` be the graph of

\[
  f_i:R_i\longrightarrow E,
  \qquad R_i\in|k_iH|,
\]

where

```text
i       k_i       genus(R_i)       deg(f_i)
1         1            2               3
2         1            2               3
3         4           17               6
```

The graph-coincidence certificates make the three curves disjoint in `J`.
Once a smooth spectral surface contains them, they are disjoint Cartier
divisors on that surface.

Since `K_J=0` and `[C]=9H+3D0`, adjunction gives

\[
  \Gamma_i^2
   =2g(R_i)-2-C\cdot\Gamma_i
   =2k_i^2-18k_i-3d_i.                                \tag{2}
\]

Therefore

\[
  (\Gamma_1^2,\Gamma_2^2,\Gamma_3^2)=(-25,-25,-58),  \tag{3}
\]

and every mixed graph intersection is zero.

## 4. Exact Prym Arithmetic

For a graph divisor expression

\[
  q=\sum_i n_i\Gamma_i+p^*(aH),
\]

the norm, ordinary Gysin, and square rows are

\[
\begin{aligned}
  \operatorname{Nm}(q)/H
    &=\sum_i n_i k_i+3a,\\
  [j_*q]_{H^2}
    &=\frac12\sum_i n_i d_i+9a,\\
  q^2
    &=\sum_i n_i^2\Gamma_i^2
      +2\sum_{i<j}n_in_j(\Gamma_i\cdot\Gamma_j)
      +4a\sum_i n_i k_i+6a^2.                         \tag{4}
\end{aligned}
\]

Insert

\[
  (n_1,n_2,n_3)=(-1,-1,-1),\qquad a=2.               \tag{5}
\]

Then

\[
\begin{aligned}
  \sum_i n_i k_i+3a
    &=-(1+1+4)+6=0,\\
  \frac12\sum_i n_i d_i+9a
    &=-\frac12(3+3+6)+18=12,\\
  q^2
    &=(-25-25-58)+8(-6)+24=-132.                      \tag{6}
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus the ordinary algebraic line `Q=O_C(q)` satisfies

\[
  \operatorname{Nm}(Q)=O_{K3},\qquad
  j_*q=12H^2,\qquad
  j_*(q^2)=-66H^2D_0.                                 \tag{7}
\]

The last equality uses `integral_J H^2D0=2`.

The physical `-9 gamma x` term is not an additional graph-period equation.
The spectral-support transgression theorem proves that it is the universal
twisted pushforward contribution forced by `9H+3D0`. Consequently (7) is the
complete ordinary graph-Prym row; the transgression supplies the separate
twisted term exactly once.

